  "grammar_points": [
    {
      "name": "〜ば いい (if you do... it's fine)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Combine the ば-conditional with いい (ii, 'good'). This gives soft advice — 'you should just do X' or 'it would be good if you did X'.\n\nExample from this song: 進めばいい (susumeba ii) = 'just move forward and it'll be fine' — from 進む (susumu, 'to advance') → 進めば (susumeba, ば-conditional) + いい (ii, 'good')\n\nMore examples:\n• 聞く (kiku, 'to ask') → 聞けばいい (kikeba ii, 'you should just ask')\n• 休む (yasumu, 'to rest') → 休めばいい (yasumeba ii, 'you should just rest')\n• やる (yaru, 'to do') → やればいい (yareba ii, 'you should just do it')\n\nNote: This is gentle advice, not a strong command. It's like saying 'just do X, it'll work out'. The tone can range from encouraging to slightly dismissive depending on context. どうすればいい？ (dou sureba ii?, 'What should I do?') is a very common question."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "進む (susumu, 'to advance') → 進めば (susumeba, conditional) + いい (ii, 'good') → 進めばいい (susumeba ii, 'just move forward')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜はず (should be)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Place はず (hazu) after a verb (dictionary or past form), adjective, or noun + の. It expresses expectation — 'should be' or 'is supposed to'. It indicates confidence that something is true.\n\nExample from this song: 飛べるはず (toberu hazu) = 'should be able to fly' — from 飛べる (toberu, 'can fly') + はず (hazu, 'should')\n\nMore examples:\n• 来る (kuru, 'to come') → 来るはず (kuru hazu, 'should come')\n• 安い (yasui, 'cheap') → 安いはず (yasui hazu, 'should be cheap')\n• 学生 (gakusei, 'student') → 学生のはず (gakusei no hazu, 'should be a student')\n\nNote: はず expresses the speaker's confident expectation based on logic or information. はずがない (hazu ga nai) = 'there's no way' (strong denial). そんなはずはない (sonna hazu wa nai, 'that can't be right')."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "飛べる (toberu, 'can fly') + はず (hazu, 'should') → 飛べるはず (toberu hazu, 'should be able to fly')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜ないでいて (keep not doing)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Take the ない-form, add で (de), then いて (ite, te-form of いる). This requests someone to maintain a negative state — 'keep not doing X' or 'stay in the state of not having done X'.\n\nExample from this song: 閉ざさないでいて (tozasanaide ite) = 'keep it from closing / stay open' — from 閉ざす (tozasu, 'to close off') → 閉ざさない (tozasanai, negative) + でいて (de ite, 'keep being')\n\nMore examples:\n• 忘れる (wasureru, 'to forget') → 忘れないでいて (wasurenaide ite, 'keep not forgetting / don't forget')\n• 変わる (kawaru, 'to change') → 変わらないでいて (kawaranaide ite, 'keep not changing / stay the same')\n• 諦める (akirameru, 'to give up') → 諦めないでいて (akiramenaide ite, 'keep not giving up')\n\nNote: This is more specific than just ないで (naide, 'don't'). The いて adds 'stay in that state' — it's a request for ongoing non-action, not just a one-time prohibition."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "閉ざす (tozasu, 'to close off') → 閉ざさない (tozasanai, negative) + でいて (de ite, 'keep being') → 閉ざさないでいて (tozasanaide ite, 'keep it from closing')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜合う (mutual action)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Take the verb masu-stem and add 合う (au, 'to meet/match'). This creates a compound verb expressing mutual or reciprocal action — 'do X to/with each other'.\n\nExample from this song: 与え合う (atae au) = 'give to each other' — from 与える (ataeru, 'to give') → 与え (atae, masu-stem) + 合う (au). Also 信じ合う (shinji au) = 'believe in each other'.\n\nMore examples:\n• 助ける (tasukeru, 'to help') → 助け合う (tasuke au, 'help each other')\n• 愛する (aisuru, 'to love') → 愛し合う (aishi au, 'love each other')\n• 話す (hanasu, 'to talk') → 話し合う (hanashi au, 'discuss together')\n\nNote: 合う as an auxiliary always implies reciprocity or mutuality. It's a beautiful and very Japanese concept — emphasizing togetherness and shared action."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "与える (ataeru, 'to give') → 与え (atae, masu-stem) + 合う (au, 'each other') → 与え合う (atae au, 'give to each other')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜としても (even if)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Add としても (to shitemo) after a verb to express a strong concessive — 'even if' or 'even assuming that'. It's more emphatic than simple ても.\n\nExample from this song: つまずいたとしても (tsumazuita to shitemo) = 'even if you stumble' — from つまずいた (tsumazuita, 'stumbled') + としても (to shitemo, 'even if')\n\nMore examples:\n• 失敗する (shippai suru, 'to fail') → 失敗したとしても (shippai shita to shitemo, 'even if I fail')\n• 嫌だ (iya da, 'dislike') → 嫌だとしても (iya da to shitemo, 'even if I hate it')\n• できない (dekinai, 'can't') → できないとしても (dekinai to shitemo, 'even if I can't')\n\nNote: としても is more hypothetical and emphatic than ても. It often introduces a scenario the speaker acknowledges but pushes past. 仮に (kari ni, 'supposing') often precedes it for extra emphasis."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "つまずく (tsumazuku, 'to stumble') → つまずいた (tsumazuita, past) + としても (to shitemo, 'even if') → つまずいたとしても (tsumazuita to shitemo, 'even if you stumble')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜ように (just like)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Place ように (you ni) after a verb to create a simile — 'just like' or 'as if'. It compares an action or state to another.\n\nExample from this song: 虹が架かるように (niji ga kakaru you ni) = 'just like a rainbow spans (the sky)' — from 虹が架かる (niji ga kakaru, 'a rainbow spans') + ように (you ni, 'just like')\n\nMore examples:\n• 鳥が飛ぶ (tori ga tobu, 'a bird flies') → 鳥が飛ぶように (tori ga tobu you ni, 'just like a bird flies')\n• 水が流れる (mizu ga nagareru, 'water flows') → 水が流れるように (mizu ga nagareru you ni, 'just like water flows')\n• 風のように (kaze no you ni, 'like the wind')\n\nNote: ように has multiple uses: simile ('like'), purpose ('so that'), and wish ('I hope'). Context determines meaning. With ますように at the end of a sentence, it becomes a prayer or wish."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "虹が架かる (niji ga kakaru, 'a rainbow spans') + ように (you ni, 'just like') → 虹が架かるように (niji ga kakaru you ni, 'just like a rainbow spans')"
    }
  ]
